-- title:
--   Chord bound and rigidity for twisted annulus profiles
-- statement:
--   Throughout, $q$ is a prime, $A$ a valuation subring of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}} =$ `AlgebraicClosure ℚ`, $N$ a nonzero natural number, $k$ an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$, and $\mathrm{red} : A \to k$ a ring homomorphism; `data : ModularPolynomialData q` is a modular polynomial datum for $q$ (a monic $\Phi \in \mathbb{Z}[X][Y]$ of degree $\psi(q)$ killing the pair $(j(\tau), j(q\tau))$) and `hKr` the Kronecker congruence $\Phi \equiv (X^q - Y)(X - Y^q)$ modulo $q$; `hα`, `hβ` assert the integrality of the two degeneracy maps $\bar\alpha, \bar\beta$ at level $(N,q)$, and $P$ is a `PlaceSpecialization` for these data, i.e. a specialisation of places of $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}\cdot X_0(N)$ to places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` together with the induced map on degree-zero divisor classes and the compatibility laws recorded in that structure. It is assumed that $q \nmid N$ (`hqN`), that $W$ is a finite set of places of `modularFunctionFieldC k N` whose members are exactly the supersingular places `ssPlaces q N k` (`hW`), and that $R$ is a `ProlongationTuple` for $P$: a pair of regular prolongations $R_1, R_2$ of $A$ to `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` with residue maps into the reduction of the level-$N$ function field, the two sheets being exchanged by the Atkin–Lehner involution.
--
--   The structural hypotheses on $R$ are: `hR`, that $R$ is a model (the two divisor laws and the two cusp laws); `hRL`, the regularity law over $W$; `hNV`, the node value law over $W$; `hO`, the fixed-order law, comparing $\mathrm{ord}$ of the pushed-forward divisor of $f$ with $\mathrm{ord}$ of the two residues at a place and its Frobenius image; and `hVI`, the value-integrality law at each $w \in W$ (every $f$ in the node integers at $w$ has $V$-value in $A$ at every place $V$ above $w$).
--
--   The datum `dat : R.AnnulusDatumLevel W` supplies, for each place $w$ of `modularFunctionFieldC k N`: a subfield $K_w \subseteq \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, node coordinates $(x_w, y_w) =$ `dat.coord w hw` over $K_w$ for $w \in W$, a width $e_w =$ `dat.width w`, a depth function $\rho$ = `dat.depthQ` on places of `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` with values in $\mathbb{Q}$, uniformisers `unifFst w`, `unifSnd w` with correction divisors `corrFst w`, `corrSnd w`, and units $u_0(w), \lambda(w), \mu(w) \in k^\times$.
--
--   The hypotheses on `dat` are the following. Widths: $1 \le e_w$ for $w \in W$ (`hwidth`), and $e_w$ equals the combinatorial width `placeWidthChar q N w` (`hwidthc`). Depths (`hdepthQ`): for $w \in W$ and every place $V$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` with `P.reduceFst V = w` that is neither strict on the first nor on the second sheet, one has $0 < \rho(V) < e_w$ and $\bigl(\mathrm{yDepth}\,V\bigr)^{\mathrm{den}\,\rho(V)} = v_A(q)^{\mathrm{num}\,\rho(V)}$ in the value group of $A$, where `yDepth` $V = v_A\bigl(V(y_w)\bigr)$. Inertia invariance of the depth (`hdepthσ`): $\rho$ is constant on orbits of the inertia subgroup `A.inertiaSubgroupIn ℚ` acting through `arithmeticGalois (modularFunctionFieldFull (N*q))`. Existence of a depth-one inertia-fixed place (`hD1`): for $w \in W$ with $2 \le e_w$ there is a place $V$ over $w$, non-strict on both sheets, fixed by the whole inertia subgroup, with $\rho(V) = 1$. Uniformisers (`hunif`): for $w \in W$, the divisor of `unifFst w` is $\mathrm{single}_w(1) + \mathrm{corrFst}\,w$ with `corrFst w` vanishing on $W$ and of degree $-1$, and the divisor of `unifSnd w` is $\mathrm{single}_{\varphi w}(1) + \mathrm{corrSnd}\,w$ with the same two properties, where $\varphi =$ `arithFrobC q k N`.
--
--   The coefficient hypotheses are: `hKfix`, the inertia subgroup fixes $K_w$ pointwise for $w \in W$; `hK`, each $K_w$ is finite over $\mathbb{Q}$; families $\varpi_w, \varepsilon_w$ in the coefficient ring `NodeLocalized.coeffSubring A (dat.K w)` $= A \cap K_w$, natural numbers $e^K_w$, and elements $u_w$ of the node integers `R.nodeIntegersOver (dat.K w) w` over $K_w$ at $w$, subject to: `hϖ`, the kernel of `NodeLocalized.redRestrict red (dat.K w)` is the principal ideal generated by $\varpi_w$; `heK`, $1 \le e^K_w$; `hε`, $\varepsilon_w$ is a unit; `hqϖ`, $q = \varpi_w^{e^K_w}\varepsilon_w$; `hε1`, $\varepsilon_w$ reduces to $1$; `hu`, $u_w$ is a unit and $x_w y_w = \bigl(\mathrm{nodeConst}\,\varpi_w\bigr)^{e_w e^K_w} u_w$; `hmax`, the ideal generated by $\varpi_w, x_w, y_w$ is maximal and is the only maximal ideal of the node integers; `hbr`, the ideals generated by $(\varpi_w, x_w)$ and by $(\varpi_w, y_w)$ are prime, with $y_w$ outside the first and $x_w$ outside the second; `hnoeth`, the node integers at $w$ are Noetherian; `hres`, for every $g$ in the node integers there is a coefficient $o$ with $g - \mathrm{nodeConst}\,o$ not a unit. The three normalisation hypotheses `hu0`, `hlam`, `hmu` state that, for $w \in W$, the first-sheet node residue of $u_w$ has value $u_0(w)$ at $w$, the first-sheet node residue of $y_w$ divided by `unifFst w` has value $\lambda(w)$ at $w$, and the second-sheet node residue of $x_w$ divided by `unifSnd w` has value $\mu(w)$ at $\varphi w$; here `HasValue` means the element lies in the valuation subring of the place and reduces to the image of the given scalar.
--
--   Next, $D$ is a degree-zero divisor on `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` over $\overline{\mathbb{Q}}$, assumed inertia-stable (`hDstab`) and supported on places that are strict on the first sheet, strict on the second sheet, or reduce into $W$ (`hDsupp`). A twist vector $a = (a_Z, a_{Z'}, a_E)$ of type `ProlongationTuple.TwistVectorLevel W` is given, with chain values $\mathrm{ch}_a(w,d)$ equal to $a_Z$ for $d = 0$, to $a_{Z'}$ for $d \ge e_w$, and to $a_E(w,d)$ otherwise. The hypothesis `ha` states that $a$ is a twist of $D$: the degree of `P.fstDiv D` is $-\sum_{w \in W} o_1(w)$, the degree of `P.sndDiv D` is $-\sum_{w \in W} o_2(w)$, and for $w \in W$ and $1 \le d \le e_w - 1$ the tent-weighted circle degree $\mathrm{circleDeg}\,D\,w\,d = \sum_{V} D(V)\max(0, 1 - |\rho(V) - d|)$ (over non-strict $V$ above $w$) equals $-\bigl(\mathrm{ch}_a(w,d-1) - 2\,\mathrm{ch}_a(w,d) + \mathrm{ch}_a(w,d+1)\bigr)$. Here the end orders are $o_1(w) = \mathrm{endOrderFst}\,a\,D\,w = \bigl(\mathrm{ch}_a(w,1) - \mathrm{ch}_a(w,0)\bigr) + \mathrm{endShareFst}\,D\,w$ and $o_2(w) = \mathrm{endOrderSnd}\,a\,D\,w = \bigl(\mathrm{ch}_a(w,e_w-1) - \mathrm{ch}_a(w,e_w)\bigr) + \mathrm{endShareSnd}\,D\,w$, the end shares being the numerators of the circle degrees at depths $0$ and $e_w$ when those rationals are integers and $0$ otherwise. Furthermore `hadm` states that the glued datum `dat.spData a D` — consisting of the two reduced divisors $\mathrm{red}_*(\mathrm{fstDiv}\,D) - \sum_{w} o_1(w)\,\mathrm{corrFst}\,w$ and $\mathrm{red}_*(\mathrm{sndDiv}\,D) - \sum_{w} o_2(w)\,\mathrm{corrSnd}\,w$ together with the node unit `dat.nodeUnitOf a D` — is admissible for the node pairs $\{(w, \varphi w)\}_{w \in W}$, that is, both divisors have degree zero and vanish at the two components of each node pair; and `hsp` states that its class in `GluedPic0` of those node pairs vanishes.
--
--   Finally a Jacobi section is given: natural numbers $d_1, d_2$, families $Q_1 : \mathrm{Fin}\,d_1 \to$ places and $Q_2 : \mathrm{Fin}\,d_2 \to$ places with every $Q_1(i)$ strict on the first sheet (`hQ₁`) and every $Q_2(j)$ strict on the second sheet (`hQ₂`); a divisor $E \ge 0$ (`hE0`); and $f \ne 0$ in `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` whose divisor is $E - \sum_i Q_1(i) - \sum_j Q_2(j) - D$, place by place (`hdivf`). The last hypothesis `hker` states that the kernel of $\mathrm{red}$ is the maximal ideal of $A$.
--
--   Under these hypotheses there exists a rational number $\delta$ with the following four properties.
--
--   First: for every $c \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $c\cdot f$ in the valuation subring $R_1$.`integers` and with nonzero first-sheet residue, and every $w \in W$,
--   $$\delta \le e_w\bigl(\mathrm{ord}_w(\mathrm{R.residue₁}(c\cdot f)) + o_1(w)\bigr).$$
--
--   Second: for every $c \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $c\cdot f$ in $R_2$.`integers` and with nonzero second-sheet residue, and every $w \in W$,
--   $$-e_w\bigl(\mathrm{ord}_{\varphi w}(\mathrm{R.residue₂}(c\cdot f)) + o_2(w)\bigr) \le \delta.$$
--
--   Third (rigidity): if $\delta = 0$ then for all $c_1, c_2 \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ such that $c_1 \cdot f$ lies in $R_1$.`integers` with nonzero residue and $c_2 \cdot f$ lies in $R_2$.`integers` with nonzero residue, and for every $w \in W$ at which $\mathrm{ord}_{\varphi w}(\mathrm{R.residue₂}(c_2\cdot f)) + o_2(w) = 0$, one has $\mathrm{ord}_w(\mathrm{R.residue₁}(c_1\cdot f)) + o_1(w) = 0$ and $E(V) = 0$ for every place $V$ of `modularFunctionFieldBar (N*q)` with `P.reduceFst V = w`.
--
--   Fourth (coupling): $\delta = 0$ holds if and only if for every $c_1 \in \overline{\mathbb{Q}}$ with $c_1 \cdot f$ in $R_1$.`integers` and nonzero first-sheet residue, the scaled element $\bigl(c_1 q^{\,a_{Z'} - a_Z}\bigr)\cdot f$ lies in $R_2$.`integers` and has nonzero second-sheet residue.
--
--   This is the concavity (chord-versus-end-tangent) step in the study of the Gauss valuation profile of a function on the supersingular annuli of $X_0(Nq)$ in characteristic $q$: a single rational slope $\delta$ bounds the first-sheet end orders from below and the second-sheet end orders from above, with equality forcing both end orders to be extremal and $E$ to carry no mass over the corresponding supersingular place, and with $\delta = 0$ equivalent to the explicit coupling of admissible scalings on the two sheets by the factor $q^{a_{Z'} - a_Z}$. It is used by [`ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.AnnulusDatumLevel.exists_endOrder_ineq_and_coupledScalings_hasValue_of_isTwistOf_of_mk_spData_eq_zero_of_inertiaStable`](thm.html#ModularCurve.PlaceSpecialization.ProlongationTuple.AnnulusDatumLevel.exists_endOrder_ineq_and_coupledScalings_hasValue_of_isTwistOf_of_mk_spData_eq_zero_of_inertiaStable).
